# cebuano-question-words.kb - Cebuano / Question words
# Format: concept | intent | answer | keywords | patterns | tl | bis
# 4 entries, one genuinely distinct fact per line.
unsa | definition | Unsa means what, and it is the main question word in Cebuano. Its variants include unsaon for how and unsaon nga for why, and unsang is the form that attaches to a noun as in unsang akong palay, which would be mine. It is the counterpart of kinsa, which asks about a person rather than a thing. | what, unsa, how, which, question | unsa meaning, unsaon meaning | unsa | ano
kinsa | definition | Kinsa means who, and it asks about a person. It is the counterpart of unsa, which asks about a thing, and the two are the basic question pair in Cebuano. Kinsa can also mean whose, so kinsa niya means who is it rather than whose it is, depending on the rest of the sentence. | who, kinsa, person, whose | kinsa meaning, unsa o kinsa | kinsa | sino
ngano | definition | Ngano means why, and it is the word for asking for a reason. It is sometimes written ngano man, where the man adds contrast, so ngano man becomes a challenge rather than a plain question. As a question word it sits with unsa for what, kinsa for who and kanus-a for when. | why, ngano, reason, question | ngano meaning, ngano man meaning | ngano | bakit
asa | definition | Asa means where, and it asks about a place. It is shorter than the Tagalog saan and the Cebuano form is also used in fixed expressions such as asa ka meaning where are you from. Like the other Cebuano question words it can stand alone as a follow-up meaning and go on. | where, asa, place, location | asa meaning, asa ka meaning | asa | saan
